---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const project = {
  title: "Fake News Detection with Classical NLP",
  subtitle:
    "Supervised text classification of real vs. fake news using TF–IDF features and linear models, with interpretability-focused analysis.",
  tags: ["Data Science"],
  stackShort: ["Python", "scikit-learn", "TF-IDF", "Logistic Regression", "SVM", "Data Visualization", "NLP", "Machine Learning"],
  poster: "/images/fake-news-static.png",
  video: null,
  links: [
    // { label: "Notebook", href: "https://github.com/..." },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title="Fake News Detection">
  <header class="project-header">
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>
    <p class="project-subtitle">{project.subtitle}</p>

    <div class="tag-row">
      {project.tags.map((tag) => (
        <span class={`badge badge-${tag.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, "-")}`}>{tag}</span>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="tag-row tag-row-tech">
      {project.stackShort.map((item) => (
        <span class="tag-pill">{item}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
  </header>

  {!project.video && project.poster && (
    <section class="project-media-block">
      <figure class="project-figure">
        <img
          class="project-image"
          src="/images/fake-news-top-fake.png"
          alt={project.title}
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="project-caption">
          The top words that indicate an article might be fake news, both biased and unbiased, for each method.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="project-section">
  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    This project looks at a straightforward but important question: how well can we distinguish
    between real and fake news articles using text alone? I've been wanting to get into NLP for a while and it seemed particularly relevant to look into fake news detection.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rather than jumping straight to large neural models, I focused on
    classical NLP approaches that are fast to train, easy to inspect,
    and still surprisingly effective. The emphasis here is on understanding what signals the models are actually using, as well as performance.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="project-section">
  <h2>Dataset</h2>
  <p>
    The dataset consists of labeled real and fake news articles. Each article was treated as a
    standalone document and converted into numerical features using TF–IDF vectorization.
    This representation captures which words are distinctive within the corpus,
    while remaining simple enough to reason about directly.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Preprocessing</h3>
    <ul class="project-bullets">
      <li>Text normalization and tokenization</li>
      <li>Stopword removal</li>
      <li>TF–IDF feature construction</li>
      <li>Train/test split for evaluation</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="project-section">
  <h2>Models</h2>
  <p>
    I trained two linear classifiers that are commonly used as strong baselines for
    text classification tasks: logistic regression and a
    support vector machine (SVM).
  </p>
  <p>
    Using linear models keeps the comparison clean: both models operate on the same
    TF–IDF feature space, making differences in behavior easier to interpret.
  </p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Logistic Regression</h3>
      <ul class="project-bullets">
        <li>Probabilistic linear classifier</li>
        <li>Fast training and stable performance</li>
        <li>Directly interpretable feature weights</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Support Vector Machine</h3>
      <ul class="project-bullets">
        <li>Margin-based classifier</li>
        <li>Well-suited to high-dimensional text data</li>
        <li>Often slightly stronger separation between classes</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="results-section">
  <h2>How the Fake News Detector Behaves</h2>

  <p class="results-intro">
    I’m not trying to “wow” anyone with a black box here — the whole point was to build a strong, inspectable baseline.
    These figures show (1) how often the models get things wrong, (2) what happens when I try to reduce dataset bias,
    and (3) which words the linear models are actually using as evidence for “fake” vs “real”.
  </p>

  <div class="results-grid">
    <figure class="results-card">
      <a
        class="results-zoom"
        href="#"
        data-full="/images/fake-news-confusion_matrices.png"
        data-alt="Confusion matrices for Logistic Regression and Linear SVM (biased vs debiased)"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/fake-news-confusion_matrices.png"
          alt="Confusion matrices for Logistic Regression and Linear SVM (biased vs debiased)"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <span class="zoom-hint">Click to expand</span>
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Confusion matrices for both models, before and after debiasing. This is the “where do they fail?” view.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="results-card">
      <a
        class="results-zoom"
        href="#"
        data-full="/images/fake-news-metrics_comparison.png"
        data-alt="Accuracy and F1 score comparison across models (biased vs debiased)"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/fake-news-metrics_comparison.png"
          alt="Accuracy and F1 score comparison across models (biased vs debiased)"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <span class="zoom-hint">Click to expand</span>
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Accuracy and F1 (fake = 1) across models. Useful for comparing “headline performance” without losing the plot.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="results-card">
      <a
        class="results-zoom"
        href="#"
        data-full="/images/fake-news-top-fake.png"
        data-alt="Top fake-leaning TF–IDF features (LogReg/SVM, biased vs debiased)"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/fake-news-top-fake.png"
          alt="Top fake-leaning TF–IDF features (LogReg/SVM, biased vs debiased)"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <span class="zoom-hint">Click to expand</span>
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        “Fake-leaning” features: the words/phrases that push predictions toward fake news in each model.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="results-card">
      <a
        class="results-zoom"
        href="#"
        data-full="/images/fake-news-top-true.png"
        data-alt="Top real-leaning TF–IDF features (LogReg/SVM, biased vs debiased)"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/fake-news-top-true.png"
          alt="Top real-leaning TF–IDF features (LogReg/SVM, biased vs debiased)"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <span class="zoom-hint">Click to expand</span>
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        “Real-leaning” features: terms associated with more conventional reporting (dates, institutions, attribution).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="project-section">
  <h2>Evaluation</h2>
  <p>
    Both models achieved strong performance on the test set.
    The SVM slightly outperformed logistic regression, though the difference was modest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rather than focusing only on headline accuracy, I looked closely at the
    confusion matrices to understand where and how the models failed.
  </p>
<p>
A good next step would also be to write an AI-writing detector, since that is a more prevalent issue.
</p>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>What I paid attention to</h3>
    <ul class="project-bullets">
      <li>Balance between false positives and false negatives</li>
      <li>Consistency of predictions across samples</li>
      <li>Qualitative differences in decision boundaries</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>




<section class="project-section">
  <h2>Interpretability & NLP insight</h2>
  <p>
    One advantage of linear models is that their decisions can be inspected directly.
    By examining feature weights, it’s possible to see which words most strongly
    influenced the classification.
  </p>

  <ul class="project-bullets">
    <li>
      Fake news articles tended to rely more on emotionally charged or attention-grabbing language
    </li>
    <li>
      Real news articles more often referenced dates, institutions, or named sources
    </li>
    <li>
      These patterns align with known stylistic differences in misinformation
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="project-muted">
    While this is a simple NLP pipeline by modern standards, it captures the core idea:
    transform text into a numerical representation, learn a decision boundary,
    and interpret what the model has learned.
  </p>
</section>


<section class="project-section">
  <h2>What this demonstrates</h2>
  <p>
    This project shows that classical NLP methods remain strong baselines for text classification,
    particularly when interpretability matters.
  </p>
  <p>
    More broadly, it reflects an end-to-end data science workflow:
    preprocessing raw text, building features, comparing models,
    evaluating results, and asking what the model is actually doing.
  </p>
</section>


  {project.links?.length > 0 && (
    <section class="project-section">
      <h2>Links</h2>
      <ul class="project-bullets">
        {project.links.map((l) => (
          <li>
            <a href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              {l.label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  )}

  <p class="back-link">← <a href="/projects">Back to Projects</a></p>
</BaseLayout>